.TH LOG_FATAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
log_fatal \- emit a log line at FATAL level with a backtrace, then terminate the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clogger.h>

log_fatal(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a structured log line at CLOG_FATAL severity, appends a full
stack backtrace, then terminates the calling process by invoking
exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  This macro never returns to the caller.

Unlike all other log_* macros, log_fatal bypasses the logger's
min_level filter.  The log line and backtrace are always written,
regardless of the logger's current minimum level (including CLOG_OFF),
so the cause of process termination is never silently suppressed.

The log line and backtrace are written inside the per-logger mutex so
they always appear contiguously in the output, even when other threads
are logging concurrently.

In CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the backtrace is embedded as an inline "bt"
array inside the JSON object (same as log_error / log_alert).  In
logfmt and syslog modes it is emitted as tab-indented continuation
lines following the main record.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Backtrace requires the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  On
platforms without execinfo.h the backtrace lines are silently omitted
but the process still terminates.

Because log_fatal terminates the process immediately, any logger
handles that have not been closed are not explicitly freed.  This is
intentional: on program exit the OS reclaims all resources.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
log_fatal(lg, "unrecoverable state: %s", reason);

log_fatal(lg, "assertion failed: invariant violated at step %d", step);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR log_alert (3),
.BR log_error (3),
.BR _clog_write (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3)
